\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Example}
\title{The compact four-dimensional Spin group is a pair of unit quaternions}

\p{For #{Q_{4,0}=x_0^2+x_1^2+x_2^2+x_3^2}, let
#{I=e_0e_1e_2e_3}. Then #{I^2=1}, #{\operatorname{rev}(I)=I}, and #{I}
commutes with the even algebra
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.prod_map_ι_sq_scalar}
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.prod_map_ι_mul_ι_of_even_length}.
Let #{H_0} be the quaternion subalgebra with basis
#{1,e_0e_1,e_0e_2,e_1e_2}, and choose
#{\theta_0:H_0\simeq\mathbb H} with the quaternion axes of
\ref{fcap-002H}:

##{e_0e_1\longmapsto-k,\qquad
e_0e_2\longmapsto i,\qquad
e_1e_2\longmapsto j.}

The eight even basis monomials show uniquely that every even value is
#{u+Iv} with #{u,v\in H_0}. Centrality and #{I^2=1} make

##{\Phi(u+Iv)
=\bigl(\theta_0(u)+\theta_0(v),\theta_0(u)-\theta_0(v)\bigr)}

an algebra map. Its inverse is

##{(q_+,q_-)
\longmapsto
\theta_0^{-1}\left(\frac{q_++q_-}{2}\right)
+I\theta_0^{-1}\left(\frac{q_+-q_-}{2}\right).}

These formulas prove both multiplicativity and bijectivity.}

\p{Reversal becomes quaternion conjugation in each factor. If
#{\mathbb H^1=\{q\in\mathbb H:\bar q q=1\}} and
#{\Phi(x)=(q_+,q_-)}, then

##{\Phi(\operatorname{rev}(x)x)
=(\bar q_+q_+,\bar q_-q_-),\qquad
\Spin(Q_{4,0})\simeq\mathbb H^1\times\mathbb H^1.}

The forward restriction lands in the displayed product. Conversely, the
written algebra inverse sends a pair of unit quaternions to an even unit of
reverse norm one, and the dimension-four carrier lemma places it in the
actual Spin group. No connectedness or covering-space argument is used.}

\p{Gallier computes the same #{u+Iv\mapsto(u+v,u-v)} decomposition, norm
equations, and compact group product using negative-square compact generators
\citet{pp. 29--30}{gallier2014clifford}. Lawson--Michelsohn independently
give the compact even-algebra reduction
\citet{I.3, Theorem 3.7, pp. 23--24}{lawson2016spin}
and the #{\mathbb H\oplus\mathbb H} entry
\citet{I.4, Table I, p. 28}{lawson2016spin}.
Their relation #{v^2=-q(v)} and the index translation of \ref{fcap-0011}
account for the sign change. The axis labels above are those fixed in
\ref{fcap-002H},
not labels imported from either source. Finally, #{(1\pm I)/2} are the two
nonzero complementary central idempotents, so the even center is the split
algebra #{\mathbb R\oplus\mathbb R}.}
